      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The health care system is better organised to react to acute rather than chronic presentations of disease, yet a significant proportion of health care expenditure is on chronic diseases and their complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This article seeks to argue the case for the organised care of chronic disease. Asthma is used as the demonstration disease because a model has already been developed and a pilot evaluation has been done."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "The National Asthma Campaign has been involved in the development, by general practitioners, of a system of asthma care organised in a way appropriate for general practice in Australia. The care is provided over a series of visits and ensures that all of the six steps of the Asthma Management Plan are covered."
        }
      ]},
